### Laravel Version

13.23.0

### PHP Version

8.5

### Database Driver & Version

_No response_

### Description

This is very likely a cause of security vulnerabilities in many applications.

Auth::guard('sanctum')->user() does not reliably provide the logged in user when nestled in with framework code. Instead, the first user in the system (very often a privileged admin) is returned instead. This is absolutely not expected behaviour and is very hard to debug.

This issue also finds the same thing: https://github.com/laravel/framework/issues/51257

The below code from Laravel\Sanctum\Guard is where the problem can be seen.

    /**
     * Retrieve the authenticated user for the incoming request.
     *
     * @param  \Illuminate\Http\Request  $request
     * @return mixed
     */
    public function __invoke(Request $request)
    {
        foreach (Arr::wrap(config('sanctum.guard', 'web')) as $guard) {
            if ($user = $this->auth->guard($guard)->user()) {
                return $this->supportsTokens($user)
                    ? $user->withAccessToken(new TransientToken)
                    : $user;
            }
        }

        if ($token = $this->getTokenFromRequest($request)) {
            $model = Sanctum::$personalAccessTokenModel;

            $accessToken = $model::findToken($token);

            if (! $this->isValidAccessToken($accessToken) ||
                ! $this->supportsTokens($accessToken->tokenable)) {
                return;
            }

            $tokenable = $accessToken->tokenable->withAccessToken(
                $accessToken
            );

            event(new TokenAuthenticated($accessToken));

            if ($this->trackLastUsedAt) {
                $this->updateLastUsedAt($accessToken);
            }

            return $tokenable;
        }
    }

The correct $accessToken is found, but the loading of the tokenable relation is broken. Instead of querying the specific user id based on the polymorphic relation, it instead grabs every single user and then takes the first result. This fallback behaviour is dangerous in itself, where some kind of sole() check would have made this issue much more discoverable.

### Steps To Reproduce

The simplest way to trigger this issue is the following:

`Relation::noConstraints(fn() => dd(Auth::guard('sanctum')->user()));`

Because the result of Auth::guard('sanctum')->user() is cached, if the query is run anywhere else first, it will give the correct result.

In typical project code, this situation happens if getting the user inside of a relationship. In my project I found it doing something along these lines. This implicitly runs the query inside Relation::noConstraints()


```
final class Workspace extends Model
{
    /**
     * All private documents stored in this workspace.
     *
     * @return HasMany<PrivateDocument, $this>
     */
    public function privateDocuments(): HasMany
    {
        return $this->hasMany(PrivateDocument::class);
    }

    /**
     * The private documents owned by the authenticated user.
     *
     * @return HasMany<PrivateDocument, $this>
     */
    public function myPrivateDocuments(): HasMany
    {
        return $this->privateDocuments()
            ->where('owner_id', Auth::guard('sanctum')->id());
    }
}

```

With relations setup like this, this query will give you someone elses private documents.

```
Workspace::query()
    ->with('myPrivateDocuments')
    ->firstOrFail();
```

### Laravel Version

13.4.0

### PHP Version

8.5.5 (phpredis 6.3.0)

### Database Driver & Version

Redis 7.4.8 (cache store), MySQL 8.0

### Description

`RedisTaggedCache::forever()` registers the entry in the tag set *before* it writes the value:

```php
public function forever($key, $value)
{
    $key = enum_value($key);

    $this->tags->addEntry($this->itemKey($key));

    return parent::forever($key, $value);
}
```

`flush()` deletes exactly what the tag set lists — its Lua script does `DEL` on the key and `ZREM` on the member.

If a `flush()` from another request lands between those two statements:

1. it reads our member from the tag set,
2. `DEL` is a no-op, because the value key does not exist yet,
3. `ZREM` removes the reference,
4. our `SET` then writes the value.

The result is a key that exists in Redis but is referenced by no tag set. Since `flush()` can only delete what the tag set lists, **no subsequent invalidation can ever reach that key**. Written through `forever()` / `rememberForever()` it also carries no TTL, so it keeps serving its stale value indefinitely.

`put()`, `add()`, `increment()` and `decrement()` register the tag entry before the write in the same way.

This is the mirror image of the problem that was fixed on the flush side: `flush()` no longer deletes the tag set key, and its `DEL` plus `ZREM` are atomic. The write path still performs its two operations in the order that makes a concurrent flush harmful.

#### Expected behaviour

Every key that exists in the keyspace should be reachable by an invalidation, so the number of entries not referenced by any tag set should be 0.

#### Impact

This is not only a keyspace leak — the orphaned entry keeps being served. In our case (an appointment booking system) the cached availability query for one department and one day became permanently stale, and a fully booked time slot kept being offered as free. Exactly one entry out of ~800 was affected, which made it look random and unreproducible. It only became visible by scanning the keyspace and comparing every key against the tag-set members.

A restart or a full `cache:clear` makes the symptom disappear, and it then takes weeks to build up again — which is why this is easy to misdiagnose as fixed by an unrelated change.

The stored values themselves are never wrong. In the runs below each entry stored its own key name as its value; all 1,767,563 reads returned either the correct value or a miss, and every remaining key still held the value it was written with. The entries are not corrupted, they are unreachable — which is why this surfaces as "stale forever" rather than as garbage data.

#### Proposed fix

Write the value first, register it afterwards:

```diff
     public function forever($key, $value)
     {
         $key = enum_value($key);

-        $this->tags->addEntry($this->itemKey($key));
+        $result = parent::forever($key, $value);

-        return parent::forever($key, $value);
+        if ($result) {
+            $this->tags->addEntry($this->itemKey($key));
+        }
+
+        return $result;
     }
```

```diff
     public function put($key, $value, $ttl = null)
     {
         $key = enum_value($key);

         if (is_null($ttl)) {
             return $this->forever($key, $value);
         }

         $seconds = $this->getSeconds($ttl);

-        if ($seconds > 0) {
-            $this->tags->addEntry(
-                $this->itemKey($key),
-                $seconds
-            );
-        }
+        $result = parent::put($key, $value, $ttl);

-        return parent::put($key, $value, $ttl);
+        if ($result && $seconds > 0) {
+            $this->tags->addEntry($this->itemKey($key), $seconds);
+        }
+
+        return $result;
     }
```

`add()`, `increment()` and `decrement()` need the same treatment.

A `flush()` running before the registration cannot delete a key it does not know about, and by the time the key is visible to a flush it is registered. Every interleaving then ends in either *no key* or *key plus tag member* — the state *key without tag member* becomes unreachable.

What remains is an ordinary cache-aside race, where the stored value may be one generation old. That value is invalidatable, so the next `flush()` clears it. As a side effect the tag entry is now only registered after a write that actually succeeded.

**Residual:** a process crash between the two statements still leaves an orphan. Closing that too would require `SET` and `ZADD` in a single Lua script, mirroring what `flush()` already does for its own two operations. The reordering alone turns a frequent concurrency hazard into a rare crash hazard.

#### History

This opens a new issue as suggested in #47903, which reported the same symptom and was closed with *"if this issue can be definitively identified as a bug, feel free to open up a new issue with a link to the original one"*. That report showed the signature precisely: `flush()` returns `true` and the key survives, while `forget()` on the same key works — an entry that no tag set references.

The flush side of this problem was analysed in #48078: back then `flush()` deleted the tag set key itself, so entries written concurrently were orphaned. Today's implementation no longer does that. What has never been looked at is the write side, which is what this report is about — and that is why reports of the same symptom kept coming in after the flush side was addressed.

Other reports of the same symptom:

- #58047 — a single key under a tag stops invalidating while all others under the same tag work; closed without a root cause, the reporter worked around it by renaming the tag.
- Discussion #46175 — tag sets growing indefinitely.
- Discussion #49159 — general concerns about the Redis tagged cache implementation.

The workarounds people converged on in those threads — capping every tagged entry at a one-hour TTL, or switching to an alternative cache package — are consistent with this cause: a TTL does not prevent the orphan, it just lets it expire on its own.

### Steps To Reproduce

Drop this command into a stock Laravel app with `CACHE_STORE=redis`. It works with both phpredis and predis:

```php
<?php

namespace App\Console\Commands;

use Illuminate\Console\Command;
use Illuminate\Redis\Connections\PhpRedisConnection;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Cache;

class TagRaceRepro extends Command
{
    protected $signature = 'tag-race {mode : write, flush or check} {--seconds=8}';

    public function handle(): int
    {
        $store = Cache::store('redis')->getStore();
        $connection = $store->connection();
        $prefix = $store->getPrefix();
        $deadline = microtime(true) + (float) $this->option('seconds');

        if ($this->argument('mode') === 'write') {
            $count = 0;
            while (microtime(true) < $deadline) {
                Cache::tags(['race'])->rememberForever('key-'.$count++, fn () => 'payload');
            }
            $this->info("wrote {$count} entries");

            return 0;
        }

        if ($this->argument('mode') === 'flush') {
            $count = 0;
            while (microtime(true) < $deadline) {
                Cache::tags(['race'])->flush();
                $count++;
            }
            $this->info("flushed {$count} times");

            return 0;
        }

        $indexed = [];
        foreach ((array) $connection->zrange($prefix.'tag:race:entries', 0, -1) as $member) {
            $indexed[$member] = true;
        }

        $globalPrefix = $connection instanceof PhpRedisConnection
            ? (string) $connection->client()->getOption(\Redis::OPT_PREFIX)
            : (string) $connection->client()->getOptions()->prefix;

        $full = $globalPrefix.$prefix;
        $namespace = sha1('tag:race:entries');
        $present = [];
        $cursor = null;

        do {
            $result = $connection->scan($cursor, ['match' => $full.$namespace.'*', 'count' => 1000]);
            if (! is_array($result)) {
                break;
            }
            [$cursor, $keys] = $result;
            foreach ((array) $keys as $key) {
                $present[substr($key, strlen($full))] = true;
            }
        } while ((int) $cursor !== 0);

        $orphans = array_diff_key($present, $indexed);

        $this->line('keys present in redis : '.count($present));
        $this->line('members in tag set    : '.count($indexed));
        $this->line('orphaned entries      : '.count($orphans));

        Cache::tags(['race'])->flush();

        $survivors = 0;
        foreach (array_keys($orphans) as $item) {
            if ($connection->exists($prefix.$item)) {
                $survivors++;
            }
        }
        $this->line('surviving a fresh flush(): '.$survivors.' of '.count($orphans));

        foreach (array_keys($present) as $item) {
            $connection->del($prefix.$item);
        }
        $connection->del($prefix.'tag:race:entries');

        return 0;
    }
}
```

Then run a writer and a flusher concurrently, and check afterwards:

```bash
php artisan tag-race write & php artisan tag-race flush & wait
php artisan tag-race check
```

#### Actual result

Eight seconds of a single writer against a single flusher:

```
flushed 37797 times
wrote 24097 entries
keys present in redis : 74
members in tag set    : 1
orphaned entries      : 73
surviving a fresh flush(): 73 of 73
```

Scaling the same setup up to 8 writers, 4 flushers and 4 readers — 1,408,113 writes, 921,090 flushes and 1,767,563 reads, **4,096,766 operations** in total:

```
keys present in redis  : 201464
members in tag set     : 476
orphaned entries       : 200988
surviving final flush(): 200988
```

Every one of those 200,988 entries has no TTL and is unreachable by any future `flush()` of that tag.

#### Same reproduction with the proposed fix applied

3,756,447 writes, 2,408,532 flushes and 4,670,690 reads — **10,835,669 operations**, same harness:

```
keys present in redis  : 0
members in tag set     : 0
orphaned entries       : 0
value mismatches       : 0
surviving final flush(): 0
```

`keys present: 0` is the meaningful part here: an orphan survives every flush by definition, as the 200,988 above demonstrate. Had a single one been created during those 10.8 million operations, it would still be sitting in the keyspace.

We have been running the reordered store in production through a custom cache driver.

### Laravel Version

13.35.0 (the code is the same on the current `13.x` branch)

### PHP Version

8.4.22

### Database Driver & Version

PostgreSQL 16.14 (the second reproduction below is driver-independent)

### Description

When the `COMMIT` of a root transaction fails inside `DB::transaction()`, `ManagesTransactions::handleCommitTransactionException()` decrements `$this->transactions` but never tells the `DatabaseTransactionsManager`. Every other path that ends a transaction (`rollBack()`, `handleTransactionException()`, `handleRollBackException()`) calls `$this->transactionsManager?->rollback(...)`; this one does not, and it does not fire the `rollingBack` connection event either.

The connection is back at level 0, but the manager still holds the failed transaction as pending. For the rest of the process (a queue worker, typically):

1. `DB::afterCommit()` called **outside** any transaction no longer runs immediately. It is attached to the failed transaction's record and runs at the next successful root commit on that connection, or never, if that next transaction rolls back or the process exits.
2. Callbacks registered with `DB::afterCommit()` **inside** the failed transaction are not discarded: they run at the next successful commit. With `DB::transaction($callback, attempts: 2)` and a concurrency error at commit time, the callbacks of the failed attempt run together with those of the successful retry, so mails/jobs dispatched after commit are sent twice.
3. `DB::afterRollBack()` callbacks of the failed transaction never run, and `TransactionRolledBack` is never dispatched.

A commit can fail for ordinary reasons: a deferred constraint, a serialization failure or deadlock reported at commit, a lost connection.

Adding the two calls that `rollBack()` already makes fixes all three symptoms for us:

```php
protected function handleCommitTransactionException(Throwable $e, $currentAttempt, $maxAttempts)
{
    $this->transactions = max(0, $this->transactions - 1);

    // missing today
    $this->transactionsManager?->rollback($this->getName(), $this->transactions);
    $this->fireConnectionEvent('rollingBack');

    // ... unchanged
}
```

I can send a PR with tests if this is the direction you would take.

### Steps To Reproduce

Both snippets must run without a wrapping test transaction (no `RefreshDatabase` / `DatabaseTransactions`), so the application transaction is the root one.

**1. Driver-independent: callbacks of a failed attempt run on retry**

```php
use Illuminate\Database\Events\TransactionCommitting;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\DB;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Event;

$failOnce = true;
Event::listen(TransactionCommitting::class, function () use (&$failOnce) {
    if ($failOnce) {
        $failOnce = false;
        throw new PDOException('deadlock detected'); // treated as a concurrency error
    }
});

$attempt = 0;
$ran = [];

DB::transaction(function () use (&$attempt, &$ran) {
    $n = ++$attempt;
    DB::afterCommit(function () use (&$ran, $n) { $ran[] = "attempt {$n}"; });
}, 2);

dump($ran);
// actual:   ["attempt 1", "attempt 2"]
// expected: ["attempt 2"]
```

**2. PostgreSQL: a real failed COMMIT leaves `afterCommit` hanging**

```php
DB::statement('CREATE TEMP TABLE t (a int, CONSTRAINT t_u UNIQUE (a) DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED)');

$ran = [];

try {
    DB::transaction(function () use (&$ran) {
        DB::afterRollBack(function () use (&$ran) { $ran[] = 'rollback callback'; });
        DB::insert('INSERT INTO t VALUES (1), (1)'); // violates the constraint at COMMIT
    });
} catch (PDOException $e) {
    // commit failed, DB::transactionLevel() is 0
}

DB::afterCommit(function () use (&$ran) { $ran[] = 'outside any transaction'; });

dump($ran);
// actual:   []
// expected: ["rollback callback", "outside any transaction"]

DB::transaction(fn () => DB::select('SELECT 1'));

dump($ran);
// actual:   ["outside any transaction"]  (only now, at an unrelated commit)
```

