### Description

Since 13.39.0, returning a `JsonApiResource` collection whose resources wrap something other than a model or an array throws:

```
Error: Cannot use object of type stdClass as array
  at Illuminate/Http/Resources/DelegatesToResource.php:72
```

The cause is `AnonymousResourceCollection::loadMissingRequestedRelationships()`, added in #62152:

```php
$this->collection->pluck('resource')->whereInstanceOf(Model::class)
```

`pluck()` reads `resource` through `data_get()`. A `JsonResource` implements `ArrayAccess`, so `data_get()` calls `offsetExists('resource')` on it, and that delegates to `isset($this->resource['resource'])`. When the wrapped value is an object that doesn't implement `ArrayAccess`, PHP throws. This happens on every request, including ones with no `include` parameter.

A single resource wrapping the same object still works. 13.38.0 worked for both.

Reading the property instead of plucking it fixes it:

```php
$this->collection->map(fn ($resource) => $resource->resource)->whereInstanceOf(Model::class)
```

### Steps To Reproduce

```php
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Http\Resources\JsonApi\JsonApiResource;

class PointResource extends JsonApiResource
{
    public function toId(Request $request): string
    {
        return (string) $this->resource->id;
    }

    public function toType(Request $request): string
    {
        return 'points';
    }

    public function toAttributes(Request $request): array
    {
        return ['x' => $this->resource->x];
    }
}

Route::get('/points', fn () => PointResource::collection([(object) ['id' => 1, 'x' => 10]]));
// Error: Cannot use object of type stdClass as array

Route::get('/point', fn () => new PointResource((object) ['id' => 1, 'x' => 10]));
// {"data":{"id":"1","type":"points","attributes":{"x":10}}}
```

