### Description

With the default `'serialization' => 'json'` in `config/session.php`, a flashed `ViewErrorBag` comes back out of the session as a plain array. `TestResponseAssert::injectResponseContext()` assumes it is still an object:

https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/master/src/Illuminate/Testing/TestResponseAssert.php#L81

```php
if (! is_null($session) && $session->has('errors')) {
    return $this->appendErrorsToException($session->get('errors')->all(), $exception);
}
```

`$session->get('errors')` is an `array`, so `->all()` throws:

```
Error: Call to a member function all() on array
  at vendor/laravel/framework/src/Illuminate/Testing/TestResponseAssert.php:81
```

The practical effect is that the thrown `Error` **replaces** the original `ExpectationFailedException`. The failing assertion, the expected/actual values and the validation messages are all lost, and every such failure surfaces as the same unrelated type error — which is a genuinely hard trail to follow, because the message points at a framework internal rather than at the test.

Two things make this easy to miss:

* it only fires when an assertion is **already failing**, so a green suite never shows it;
* `assertSessionHasErrors()` handles the array shape fine, so the session data itself looks healthy.

Application code is unaffected — validation errors still render normally in the browser. This is purely the testing helper.

Worth noting that `json` is the shipped default, so this affects new applications out of the box; setting `'serialization' => 'php'` works around it but trades away the gadget-chain protection that default exists for.

A guard along the lines of

```php
$errors = $session->get('errors');

if ($errors instanceof ViewErrorBag) {
    return $this->appendErrorsToException($errors->all(), $exception);
}
```

would keep the original assertion message intact when the bag did not survive serialization. Happy to open a PR if that direction looks right.

### Steps To Reproduce

On a fresh Laravel 13 application (default `config/session.php`, so `'serialization' => 'json'`):

```php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;
use Illuminate\Validation\ValidationException;

public function test_failing_assertion_loses_its_message(): void
{
    Route::middleware('web')->post('/repro', function () {
        throw ValidationException::withMessages(['name' => 'The name field is required.']);
    });

    // Deliberately wrong target, so the assertion fails and the helper tries
    // to enrich the message with the session errors.
    $this->from('/')->post('/repro')->assertRedirect('/somewhere-else');
}
```

**Expected:** a failed assertion reporting the redirect mismatch, with the validation errors appended.

**Actual:**

```
Error: Call to a member function all() on array
  at vendor/laravel/framework/src/Illuminate/Testing/TestResponseAssert.php:81
```

Setting `'serialization' => 'php'` in `config/session.php` makes the same test report the mismatch correctly, which confirms the serialization round trip as the cause.

