### Description

I have a `belongsToMany` relation scoped with the new closure form of `wherePivot()` (added in #61150), and I used `detach()` to remove only the pivot rows matching that specific scope. Instead, `detach()` **removes all** pivot rows for the parent model.. ignoring the closure scope I defined.

Digging into it, found that `wherePivot()` handles the closure and string forms differently ([BelongsToMany.php](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Relations/BelongsToMany.php):396-413):

```php
public function wherePivot($column, $operator = null, $value = null, $boolean = 'and')
{
    if ($column instanceof Closure) {
        $pivotQuery = (new ($this->getPivotClass()))
            ->setTable($this->table)
            ->newQueryWithoutRelationships();

        $column($pivotQuery);

        $this->query->getQuery()->addNestedWhereQuery($pivotQuery->getQuery(), $boolean);

        return $this; // returns here, never touches $this->pivotWheres
    }

    $this->pivotWheres[] = func_get_args(); // string form gets recorded here

    return $this->where($this->qualifyPivotColumn($column), $operator, $value, $boolean);
}
```

`detach()`, `updateExistingPivot()`, and `newPivotStatementForId()` all go through `InteractsWithPivotTable::newPivotQuery()`, which builds its query **only** from `$this->pivotWheres` (plus `pivotWhereIns`/`pivotWhereNulls`) ([InteractsWithPivotTable.php](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Relations/Concerns/InteractsWithPivotTable.php):669-686):

```php
public function newPivotQuery()
{
    $query = $this->newPivotStatement();

    foreach ($this->pivotWheres as $arguments) {
        $query->where(...$arguments);
    }
    // ....
    return $query->where($this->getQualifiedForeignPivotKeyName(), $this->parent->{$this->parentKey});
}
```

Since the closure branch never get appended to `pivotWheres`, that condition simply doesn't exist as far as `newPivotQuery()` is concerned.. so, `detach()` operates on the *entire unscoped* pivot table for that parent, silently deleting rows it shouldn't touch. Though the string form of `wherePivot()` does not have this problem, since it already correctly records into `pivotWheres`.

This is a data-loss risk: the closure scope reads as if it restricts the relation everywhere (as the string form does, as it was supposed to), but `detach()`/`updateExistingPivot()` quietly bypass it. 

and this isn't limited to only `detach()`/`updateExistingPivot()`. `orWherePivot()` ([BelongsToMany.php](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Relations/BelongsToMany.php):490-493) also delegates to `wherePivot()`, so it hits the same closure branch and hit the same gap. 
`sync()` ([InteractsWithPivotTable.php](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Relations/Concerns/InteractsWithPivotTable.php):99) is affected too, and worse. It computes its "currently attached" set via `getCurrentlyAttachedPivots()` → `getCurrentlyAttachedPivotsForIds()` ([InteractsWithPivotTable.php](https://github.com/laravel/framework/blob/13.x/src/Illuminate/Database/Eloquent/Relations/Concerns/InteractsWithPivotTable.php):583-609), which itself calls `newPivotQuery()` - same unscoped builder `detach()` uses. So the closure scope is dropped before `sync()` even diffs against the given IDs: so a `$user->activeRoles()->sync([...])` can detach rows that were never in the scope, since the "current" set it diffs against silently includes out-of-scope rows.

### Steps To Reproduce

given a `User belongsToMany Role` setup with a pivot table `role_user` that has an `active` boolean column:

```php
// suppose role_user pivot rows for user #1: role 1 (active=1), role 2 (active=1), role 3 (active=0)

class User extends Model
{
    public function activeRoles()
    {
        return $this->belongsToMany(Role::class)
            ->wherePivot(fn ($query) => $query->where('active', 1));
    }
}
```

```php
$user = User::find(1);

$user->activeRoles()->detach();

// expected: only pivot rows where active = 1 are removed (roles 1 and 2), role 3 stays
// actual currently: all pivot rows for user 1 get removed, including role 3
```

For comparison, the equivalent string-form scope behaves correctly and only detaches the intended rows:

```php
$user->belongsToMany(Role::class)->wherePivot('active', 1)->detach();
// correctly leaves role 3 (active = 0) untouched
```

