### Description

`Route::name()->post()` and `Route::post()->name()` produce different route metadata when the controller action is not callable.
`Route::name()` returns a `RouteRegistrar` object whereas `Route::post()` returns a `Route` object.

- When the controller action is callable, `RouteRegistrar::compileAction()` converts the `[Controller::class, 'method']` action into an associative `['uses' => ..., 'controller' => ...]` array before merging the registrar attributes.
- When the controller action is not callable, the original numeric array `[0 => Controller::class, 1 => 'method']` is merged with the registrar attributes instead.
- `RouteAction::parse()` subsequently normalizes the numeric callable-like array into `['uses' => ..., 'controller' => ...]`, but the previously merged as attribute is not preserved.

As a result, the route name is lost only for the `Route::name(...)->post(...)` form when the target action is not callable and kept when target action is callable.

Should `Route::name(...)->post(...)` and `Route::post(...)->name(...)` be guaranteed to produce semantically equivalent routes, regardless of whether the target controller method exists ?

### Steps To Reproduce

```php
// app/Http/Controllers/ExistingController.php
namespace App\Http\Controllers;
class ExistingController
{
    public function foo() {}
}



// routes/web.php
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Route;
use App\Http\Controllers\ExistingController;

Route::name('test.before')        ->post('/before',         [ExistingController::class, 'foo']);
Route::name('test.before-missing')->post('/before-missing', [ExistingController::class, 'missing']);

Route::post('/after',         [ExistingController::class, 'foo'])    ->name('test.after');
Route::post('/after-missing', [ExistingController::class, 'missing'])->name('test.after-missing');

